QEMU device models for the Raspberry Pi 3B port
===============================================

Patches against qemu v10.1.3, the version these boards are tested on.
They are here because parts of the Pi 3B are either unmodelled by QEMU
or modelled in a way that cannot exercise the guest driver: without
them the corresponding kernel code is written blind.

To apply and build:

	git clone --depth 1 --branch v10.1.3 \
		https://gitlab.com/qemu-project/qemu.git
	cd qemu && git am /path/to/tools/qemu-patches/*.patch
	mkdir build && cd build
	../configure --target-list=aarch64-softmmu --disable-docs \
		--disable-fuse --disable-jack
	ninja

Run the result by explicit path; do not install over a packaged qemu.
On macOS the binary is qemu-system-aarch64-unsigned, which runs fine
under TCG.

EVIDENCE TIERS.  Results in doc/raspi3b-completion-plan.ms are labelled
by what they were checked against, strongest first:

	hardware	a real Raspberry Pi 3B
	qemu		a device model QEMU already shipped
	qemu+patch	a device model in this directory

qemu+patch is the weakest of the three and is called out wherever it is
used.  Both halves are ours there, so a passing test can mean the model
implements the driver's bug.  Two rules keep that honest, and they are
worth keeping when adding to this series:

  - write the model from the hardware documentation or an existing
    Linux/9front driver, not from what the Inferno driver happens to
    emit, and say in the patch where it deviates;

  - make the model reject protocol violations rather than tolerate
    them.  QEMU's own usb-msd answering "Bad CBW size" is what caught
    the dropped bulk maxpkt in appl/cmd/usb/usbd.b; a permissive model
    would have hidden it.

WHAT COULD GO UPSTREAM.  Two of these are plain qemu bugs, independent
of Inferno and of this port, and could be sent to qemu-devel on their
own:

	0006	cprman matched the wrong mux's divisor register.
		Affects every clock mux, so every guest.
	0008	the mini UART dropped its storage registers, so a
		driver could not read back what it had written.

0010 is a bug in 0009, not in qemu; it is kept as a separate patch
because the reasoning is worth reading.  Everything else adds a device
model qemu does not have, and only matters to someone emulating a Pi in
this much detail \- which is most of the series, and increasingly so as
it grows.  Do not read the section above as a to-do list for upstream:
two patches of twelve are general-purpose fixes and the rest are not.

THE PATCHES

0001 bcm2835_powermgt: model the watchdog countdown.

	Stock QEMU resets the machine the instant a guest sets RSTC's
	full-reset bit, and logs PM_WDOG as unimplemented.  The
	watchdog therefore cannot be armed, watched, fed or stopped,
	and os/inferno/raspi3b/pmbcm.c's arm-and-stop self-test
	reboot-loops against it.

	With the patch the counter ticks at 65536 Hz, counts only
	while RSTC selects full-reset, reads back the time remaining,
	and freezes where it stopped.  Linux's reboot path is
	unaffected: it arms with 10 ticks, about 150us.

0002 bcm2835_gpio: honour alt3 on the SD card pins.

	Stock QEMU recognises only alt0 (SDHOST) on GPIO 48-53.  The
	alt3 write that hands the card to the Arasan SDHCI block --
	what sdhcibcm.c does, and what the hardware requires -- is
	ignored, so the card stays wherever it was parented.  Since it
	is parented to SDHCI at reset, the SDHCI driver appears to
	work while its routing write does nothing, and a driver that
	omitted the write entirely would pass identically.

	With the patch alt3 selects SDHCI, so the routing is real: the
	card can be handed to SDHOST and taken back.

0003 hw/usb: model the SMSC LAN9514 ethernet controller.

	The Pi's onboard network adapter.  Stock qemu offers usb-net,
	which is CDC ECM, and nothing that answers the vendor requests
	and framing an smsc95xx-style driver uses, so the SMSC half of
	appl/cmd/usb/etherusb.b -- about a thousand lines -- had never
	executed.

	The model covers the ethernet function: vendor requests A0/A1
	for register access, the transmit command words, the receive
	status word and FCS, burst packing under MEF and the burst
	cap, and the internal PHY at address 1.  No EEPROM is fitted,
	as on a Pi, so a driver falls back to the firmware's MAC.

	Run it with:

		-device usb-lan9514,netdev=n0 -netdev user,id=n0

0004 hw/sd: model a generic SDIO function.

	qemu implements SD and eMMC memory cards and no SDIO at all,
	so sdhcibcm.c's sdiocmd52 and sdiocmd53 -- Phase 6's transport
	-- were unrunnable rather than merely untested.

	sd-io is an I/O-only function: CCCR at function 0, one I/O
	function behind it, and CMD5/CMD3/CMD7/CMD52/CMD53.  It
	answers nothing else -- CMD8, ACMD41 and CMD9 are absent on
	purpose, so a memory-card probe is refused rather than
	indulged.

	It is deliberately generic and not a BCM43430.  There is no
	ether4330 in this tree and no firmware loader, so a Wi-Fi
	model would have no consumer and would prove only that the two
	halves agree with each other.

0005 hw/gpio/bcm2835_gpio: place an I/O function, and never displace one.

	The card routing assumed the only device on these buses was
	the card.  With an SDIO function present it would reparent the
	function as though it were the card, breaking it and stranding
	the real card.  Move only a removable card, find it wherever
	it is, and refuse a bus that already holds a device; settle a
	non-card device onto the Arasan, which is the only controller
	this SoC wires one to.

	Run it with:

		-device sd-io

	The function needs no bus argument: it is settled onto the
	Arasan on the guest's first pin programming.  Attaching it
	gives the wiring a real Pi 3B has -- card on SDHOST, Wi-Fi on
	the Arasan -- so SDHCI-NONE is then the correct result, not a
	failure.

0006 hw/misc/bcm2835_cprman: match a clock mux's divisor register.

	A real qemu bug, found by writing the PWM driver.
	update_mux_from_cm() matches a mux's CM_DIV at cm_offset + 4,
	but cm_offset is a word index and CTL/DIV are adjacent words,
	so "+ 4" names the CTL register of the mux two slots on.
	CM_PWMCTL (word 40) is matched by the OTP mux's 36 + 4, so
	programming the PWM clock updated the OTP mux and returned and
	the PWM clock never started.  Affects every mux, not just PWM.

0007 hw/misc: model the BCM2835 PWM.

	PWM_OFFSET was defined and nothing was mapped there, so a
	guest reached unassigned memory.  Two channels, range and data
	registers, the shared 16-word FIFO with full/empty, the status
	register, and the serialiser clock taken from the clock
	manager's "pwm" mux so a channel enabled without a clock is
	reported rather than silently idle.

	No serialiser runs: nothing consumes the FIFO on its own and
	no waveform reaches a pin, so GAPO never comes on by itself
	and a duty cycle cannot be measured.  The registers, the FIFO
	discipline and the error reporting are modelled, which is the
	part a driver gets wrong.

	Needs no command-line argument; the block is part of the SoC.

0008 hw/char/bcm2835_aux: keep the mini UART's storage registers.

	A real qemu bug.  LCR, MCR, CNTL, SCRATCH and the baud rate
	divisor were decoded and discarded, so a driver could not read
	back what it had just written and had no way to tell a mini
	UART it had configured from one it had not.  MSR now reports
	CTS and DSR asserted, CNTL resets to 0x3 as the datasheet
	says, and a 7-bit LCR selection is reported rather than
	silently ignored.

	Needs no command-line argument, but the mini UART is a second
	serial port, so seeing its output takes a second -serial:

		-serial mon:stdio -serial file:aux.log

	Without that the AUX result still appears on the console --
	auxbcm.c reports through print() -- but nothing written to the
	mini UART itself is visible anywhere.

0009 hw/timer: model the BCM2835 ARM timer.

	create_unimp(... "bcm2835-sp804" ...) mapped nothing, and the
	datasheet is explicit that this is not an SP804: the counter
	is 23-bit rather than 32, there is a pre-divider between the
	APB clock and the counter, and there is a second free-running
	up-counter with its own prescaler.  A stock SP804 driver gets
	the countdown roughly right and the free-running counter not
	at all.

0010 hw/timer: compute the ARM timer counters from the clock.

	A bug in 0009, kept separate because the reasoning generalises.
	Driving the countdown with a ptimer and reporting
	ptimer_get_count() for VALUE reads back a hard zero for any
	access taken after the counter expired but before ptimer's
	callback ran -- hw/core/ptimer.c clamps to 0 to "prevent timer
	underflowing if it should already have triggered", whatever
	the policy flags say.  Under TCG a guest in a calibration loop
	starves the main loop, so that window is milliseconds wide,
	and a guest polling VALUE saw the counter parked at zero
	instead of wrapping.

	Both counters are now computed on demand from the virtual
	clock and a plain QEMUTimer delivers only the interrupt, so
	the value and the deadline come from one base and cannot
	drift.  include/hw/ptimer.h says new devices should not use
	PTIMER_POLICY_LEGACY; this one uses no ptimer at all.

0011 hw/audio: model the BCM2835 I2S/PCM interface.

	I2S_OFFSET was another create_unimp, so a guest could neither
	set a sample rate nor move a frame.  Both 64-word FIFOs, the
	frame and channel configuration, the thresholds, the sync
	flags, the write-one-to-clear error bits, the DMA request
	levels and the interrupt, clocked from the clock manager's pcm
	mux.  The FIFOs are advanced on demand for the reason 0010
	gives.

	No audio backend: TX data is discarded and RX reads zeroes,
	which is what an unconnected DIN pin gives on a board.

	One deliberate departure from the datasheet, and it is in a
	comment in the model too: its TXTHR and RXTHR description is
	defective -- four settings, but the text for 01 and 10 is
	identical and omits the fraction.  The hardware uses quarters.

	Needs no command-line argument; the block is part of the SoC.

0012 hw/char/bcm2835_aux: model the two AUX SPI masters.

	The AUX block holds three peripherals behind one AUX_ENABLES
	register, and only the mini UART was modelled; 0x80 and 0xc0
	fell through to "Bad offset".  Not a second copy of
	hw/ssi/bcm2835_spi.c: the shift length is 1 to 32 BITS rather
	than a whole number of bytes, and transmitted data comes from
	the top of the written word while received data arrives at the
	bottom.

	The IO and TXHOLD windows differ in whether chip select drops
	after the word, and modelling that was necessary rather than
	decorative: an m25p16 answered a JEDEC identify with zeroes
	until CS actually fell and rose, because nothing else can
	resynchronise a peripheral's command state machine.

	Attach a peripheral to exercise it:

		-drive if=none,format=raw,id=mtd0,file=flash.img \
		-device m25p16,bus=aux-spi1,cs=0,drive=mtd0

	which gives "auxspi: jedec 20 20 15".  That result is the
	strongest in the series: the flash is qemu's own model, so the
	register layer is checked against something this port did not
	write.

	No serialiser runs, as with SPI0: a transfer completes inside
	the register write that starts it, so TX_FULL never comes on
	by itself.  Variable-width mode is logged, not modelled.

SELF-TESTS

Two kernel self-tests are compiled out by default, because each can
leave the board unusable if the hardware does not behave -- on a real
Pi as much as under an emulator -- and a boot-time check that can brick
its own boot is not worth having on by default.

	-DWDOGSELFTEST	pmbcm.c: arm the watchdog, watch it count
			down, stop it.  If the stop does not take, the
			machine reboots 8 seconds later, and again
			after that.

	-DSDROUTETEST	sdhcibcm.c: route the card to SDHOST and
			back.  If it cannot be taken back, the board
			has no SD card.

	-DSDIOTEST	sdhcibcm.c: bring up an SDIO function and move
			data through it with CMD52 and CMD53, in byte
			mode and block mode.  Bringing SDIO up resets
			the Arasan, which an SD card sitting on it
			would not enjoy.

Build them by compiling the two files by hand with the define, then
relinking:

	cd os/inferno/raspi3b
	mk
	7c -wFV -DWDOGSELFTEST -DSDROUTETEST ... pmbcm.c sdhcibcm.c
	touch -t 203001010000 pmbcm.7 sdhcibcm.7
	mk

The touch is needed because mk rebuilds an object whose timestamp
merely ties with its source, and the two are written in the same
second.  It also dates the objects to 2030, so mk will never rebuild
them again.  Delete them to get back to a default kernel:

	rm -f pmbcm.7 sdhcibcm.7
	mk

Deleting the objects is not always enough on its own: mk compares the
kernel against them, and with them gone it can decide there is nothing
to do and leave the old kernel in place.  If iraspi3b still has the
self-test strings in it, force the issue:

	rm -f pmbcm.7 sdhcibcm.7 main.7 iraspi3b
	touch pmbcm.c sdhcibcm.c main.c
	mk
	strings iraspi3b | grep -c SDIO-OK	# expect 0

Do not leave a self-test kernel installed in Inferno/aarch64/bin: it
reboot-loops on stock qemu, which is a confusing artifact to leave for
someone else.

CHANGING LIMBO

A change to a .b under appl/ does not reach the kernel by rebuilding
the kernel: the board's root image is built from the installed .dis
files, and the image is only regenerated when it is missing.  So:

	cd appl/cmd/usb && mk etherusb.dis
	cp etherusb.dis $ROOT/dis/usb/
	cd $ROOT/os/inferno/raspi3b
	rm -f raspi3b.root.s raspi3b.root.7
	mk

Skipping either the copy or the rm leaves the old code running, which
looks exactly like a change that did not work.

What they print, against patched qemu with a 16M raw SD image
(-drive file=sd.img,if=sd,format=raw):

	WDOG-PRESENT loaded and holding while disarmed
	WDOG-OK armed, counted down and stopped
	sdhci: alt0 released the card, alt3 took it back
	SDROUTE-OK
	SDHCI-OK

With -DSDIOTEST and -device sd-io, on the Pi wiring (card on SDHOST,
I/O function on the Arasan):

	sdio: 1 I/O function(s), rca 0x...
	sdio: CCCR revision 0x32
	SDIO-OK CMD52 and CMD53 carried data through function 1, byte and block mode
	SDHCI-NONE (no /dev/sdH0/data: file does not exist)
	SD-OK

SDHCI-NONE is the correct result there, not a failure: the Arasan has
the I/O function, so the card stays on SDHOST.

Against stock qemu the watchdog test reboot-loops and never reaches a
banner, and the routing test reports SDROUTE-FAIL.  The default kernel,
with neither define, boots clean on both.

The PWM needs no define either.  A default kernel on patched qemu
prints:

	pwm: pwm at 0x3f20c000, serialiser clock 200000 Hz
	PWM-OK clock, mark/space, balanced, FIFO full/empty and errors

and on stock qemu prints PWM-NONE and carries on, the block being
genuinely absent there.

The LAN9514 needs no define.  With patched qemu and the device
attached, a default kernel prints:

	etherusb: smsc id_rev 0xec000002
	ETHERUSB serving /net.alt/ether mac 525400123456 (SMSC LAN95xx ep3.0)
	ETHERBIND-OK
	ETHER-DHCP-OK ip=10.0.2.15 gw=10.0.2.2
	ETHER-PING-DONE
